; A stack overflow on `baremetal-aarch64`, taken on purpose: a recursion
; with no base case. `dive` stores its depth through a volatile access,
; so no call can be removed, and it subtracts after the call, so LLVM
; can't turn it into a loop (subtraction isn't associative).
;
; The stack is 8 KiB, and the 64 KiB below it is an unmapped guard
; (docs/embedded-guide.md section 6). The first push past the stack's
; bottom is a translation fault there: a data abort at the current
; exception level (EC 0x25), a write (WnR), a level-3 translation fault
; (DFSC 0x07), so ESR_EL1 is 0x96000047 and FAR_EL1 is in the guard. The
; fault exit switches to its own stack before touching memory, reports
; the registers, adds the stack-overflow line, and exits 81.
;
; `scripts/check-embedded.sh` A17 boots it under QEMU and reads FAR
; against the image's own `__axiom_rw_end` and `__axiom_stack_lo`. With
; the guard mapped (the `guard` ablation) the stack runs on through
; `.bss` and `.data` and faults somewhere else, or not at all; with the
; MMU off, as before, it ran through the arena and into the code and
; hung.
(import IO)
(import Mem)

(:: dive (-> Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (dive cell n)
  {
    (__vstore64 cell n)
    (- (dive cell (+ n 1)) n)
  })

(:: main Int)
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (main)
  {
    (println "OVERFLOW PROBE")
    (println (dive (memAlloc 16) 0))
    (println "NOT REACHED")
    0
  })
